{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791131444,"nonce":"TsKV9NiAa6tBa63oTb29hUyx4OI=","title":"caribou_zyntax::describe fails on a ZynML module that imports a plugin","message":"`describe(root, ..)` makes a world containing only the root's Zyntax frontends. It then parses and lowers every ZynML module under the root to publish its declarations. A module that imports a plugin's class (`from math import Vec2`) finds no host module, because the world has no plugins, and its lowering fails at the first call into the class: \"Call to undefined function 'Vec2$new'\". The whole describe fails with it, so the Haxe build macro can't read any module under that root.\n\nPython modules don't hit this, because they are read by `describe_module` without lowering.\n\nWhat describe needs is the project's plugins (the cbproj's `plugins`) registered in its world, so host modules resolve as they do at run time. Alternatively, an import it can't resolve could leave its members untyped while describing.\n\nThe interop fixtures that import the math plugin from ZynML live under `fixtures/native`, outside the root `describe`'s test walks.","files":null}]}